      "source": "dossier.md §Driver Technology; optics-news-15-10-4/output.md §Silicon fuel",
      "rationale": "Frame: whole-plant fixed. One centralized target factory serves the entire 1 GWe plant; it does not replicate per module. C220108 is a Class U account — the framework multiplies the per-module value by n_mod=10 to reach the fleet cost. Dividing by n_mod ($200M / 10 = $20M/module) ensures the fleet total stays at $200M rather than scaling to ~$2B. Derivation: At 1 GWe (10 modules, 10 Hz each): 3.15B targets/year = 630,000 wafers/year (at 5,000 targets/wafer per dossier §Driver Technology). By semiconductor industry standards, 630,000 wafers/year is a mid-size fab (modern fabs process 50,000–100,000 wafers/month). A dedicated clean-room line at this throughput costs $150–300M CAPEX (not a leading-edge logic fab — the nanostructure geometry uses mature lithography nodes). Midpoint: $200M fleet total. Per-target CAPEX: $200M / 3.15B targets = $0.063/target. Contrast with 04-laser-icf: HB11's target factory (complex capacitor-coil assemblies at ~$5/target) is estimated at $157.5M for a 500 MWe plant. Marvel's silicon wafer targets eliminate per-target electromagnet assemblies and cryogenics, but the 10× higher rep rate (10 Hz vs 1 Hz) requires 10× throughput. Sensitivity range: $150M–$300M fleet.",
